| 1 | 6 | How a piece works. *How is a piece of music built, and how do its setting and history shape the way people play it?* | 18 | mu-g6-read; mu-g6-respond | MU:Pr4.2.6a; MU:Pr4.2.6b; MU:Pr4.2.6c; MU:Re7.2.6a; MU:Re7.2.6b; MU:Re9.1.6a; MU:Cn11.1.6a | Performing (Pr4); responding (Re7, Re9); connecting (Cn11). Works: "Shady Grove", a traditional Appalachian old-time song (United States), the class's performance piece, sung and played on buckets from a Curio Class lead sheet, never a copyrighted arrangement; Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov, Scheherazade, first movement (Russia, 1888), whose solo violin theme returns between sections, a public domain score in a recording whose rights line the unit plan checks; Huun-Huur-Tu (Tuva, in Siberia, Russia, a throat-singing group founded in 1992; the unit plan records whether active, as of its date), a song described and linked to the group's page; a song by Youssou N'Dour (Senegal, born 1959; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked to the artist's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: reading standard notation with mixed note values (mu-g5-read) and a response backed by evidence from the music (mu-g5-respond), each with its ten-minute reteach. MU:Pr4.2.6a: mark the form of "Shady Grove" (verse and refrain) and explain how its rhythm, melody and chord pattern work in each part before playing it. MU:Pr4.2.6b: read and name, one by one, the standard symbols for dynamics (p, mf, f, crescendo), articulation (accent, staccato, slur), pitch (treble staff notes, sharp, flat) and rhythm (quarter, eighth pairs, dotted quarter, rests). MU:Pr4.2.6c: tell how old-time music's history (tunes passed on by ear at house parties and dances, the banjo's roots in African instruments brought to the Americas, verses swapped and made up from player to player) shapes how "Shady Grove" is played. MU:Re7.2.6a: describe how elements and expression connect to a listening work's structure (Scheherazade's violin theme leaving and coming back). MU:Re7.2.6b: name the setting (when, where, by whom, for what) of music from several genres, cultures and eras, the four works and short unseen excerpts. MU:Re9.1.6a: judge a performance with the teacher's criteria (accurate, expressive, fits its style). MU:Cn11.1.6a: show how music relates to another art, a school subject, a setting or daily life (Tuvan throat singing and the herding life and landscape its sounds call up). Technique: reading a lead sheet (melody on the staff, chord letters above), the symbol cards. Words: structure, symbol, dynamics, articulation, setting, criteria. Showing: "Shady Grove" sung and played for a partner class, with a one-minute spoken note on its setting by a volunteer group. End-of-unit evidence (two check days: day 16 the reading sitting, day 17 the listening sitting), id by id: MU:Pr4.2.6a, a kept sheet made on the last rehearsal day: the class score marked with its form and one sentence on how rhythm and melody work in each part; MU:Pr4.2.6b, a written item: eight symbols on an unseen Curio Class original score, each named and its meaning told; MU:Pr4.2.6c, a written item: two sentences on how old-time music's history shapes one way "Shady Grove" is played; MU:Re7.2.6a, a written item on an unseen open-licensed recording: how its elements and expression fit its structure; MU:Re7.2.6b, a written item: three unseen excerpts matched to three setting cards, with one clue in the sound named for each; MU:Re9.1.6a, a written item: an unseen Curio Class original take judged on the teacher's three criteria, a reason for each; MU:Cn11.1.6a, a written item: one way one of the unit's works relates to another art, a subject, a setting or daily life. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: comparing two contrasting performance pieces (unit 5), reading at sight (unit 9). Tools: symbol cards, the lead sheet, work cards and the listening-log page, Curio Class's own; a notation viewer on the teacher screen or the printed score. Free path: desks and buckets for the rhythm, the voice for the melody. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 6 because every later unit reads a score and names a setting: unit 2's interpretation needs the piece's structure and symbols, and unit 3 writes with the symbols read here. The rhythm-syllables and describe-interpret-judge models are retrieved here, where first needed. 18 days: seven ids, two techniques (symbol reading, the listening response) and two check days | Candidate: a third listening work by a living Native American or Pacific Islander maker, described and linked, for range | Old-time music: the sensitivity agent reads a folk music archive's or a museum's published pages first, and checks the verses kept; the banjo's African roots and the music's mixed Black and white history are told plainly from that source; no accent put on. Tuvan throat singing: told from the group's own pages and a museum's page, read first by the agent; no student is taught or pushed to throat-sing (voice rest rule). N'Dour: the agent reads his own pages; the song's words (Wolof) are read in translation first. Hazards and controls: recordings at a set level the class can talk over; buckets and sticks at a set soft stroke, no drum near an ear; singing in a comfortable range, a changing voice chooses its octave or speaks the rhythm; clear aisles; hands washed after shared instruments. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
