ng and performing an interpretation; tone color on the voice and on found sounds. Words: interpretation, intent, tempo, rubato, tone color, purpose. Showing: the planned performances in groups or duets for the class, each with a one-line intent card. End-of-unit evidence (two check days: day 34 the performance day, day 35 the written sitting), id by id: MU:Pr4.3.6a, a performance item: the planned interpretation performed in a group or duet (no solo needed), guide look-fors on the planned dynamics, tempo and articulation heard; MU:Re8.1.6a, a written item on two unseen takes of one public domain piece: how each performer's choices carry intent; MU:Pr4.1.6a, a kept listening-log page made on the last making day: three picks for a given purpose, each explained against the teacher's criteria; MU:Re7.1.6a, a written item: one pick's tie to the student's own interests or to an invented listener's, marked the same; MU:Cn10.1.6a, a written item: how the student's tastes, knowledge or skills shaped one choice on the expression map. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: two contrasting pieces interpreted (unit 6). Tools: the expression map, the listening-log page and the purpose cards, Curio Class's own; a keyboard tool or the printed strip with the tune sung. Free path: the voice and found sounds for tone color. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 1 because an interpretation is planned on a score whose structure and symbols the student can read; before unit 3 because composing for a stated intent grows from planning intent in another's piece. The expression-map and listening-log models are retrieved here. 18 days: a rehearsed performance and a program of picks, two check days. The mid-year check follows (week 19) | Candidate: a second interpretation tradition from Africa or the Arab world, described and linked | Interests: a student's own tastes are invited, never required; the invented-listener option is marked the same, and no pick is marked down for its 