| 8 | 7 | In style. *How does a class decide when its music is ready, and what does it mean to play in style?* | 12 | mu-g7-revise; mu-g7-perform | MU:Cr3.1.7a; MU:Cr3.1.7b; MU:Cr3.2.7a; MU:Pr5.1.7a; MU:Pr6.1.7a; MU:Pr6.1.7b | Creating (Cr3); performing (Pr5, Pr6); responding (review, with class criteria). Works: a samul nori performance (Korea, a four-drum and gong form first staged in 1978 from older farmers' band music, pungmul), a living group picked by the unit plan, described and linked: an ensemble playing in the round, outdoors and on a stage; Clara Schumann, Romance Op. 22 No. 1 for violin and piano (Germany, 1853), a public domain score in a checked recording: a chamber recital; the class's own variation pieces and "Mango Walk" in its mento style | Retrieval in day 2's opener: judging against the teacher's criteria (mu-g6-revise, unit 4) and the readiness check (mu-g6-perform, unit 4). MU:Cr3.1.7a: judge the unit 7 piece against criteria the student chooses (style, form, sound sources). MU:Cr3.1.7b: explain each revision, citing the criteria and the teacher's and peers' feedback. MU:Cr3.2.7a: present the documented piece with craft, an idea of the pair's own, unity and variety. MU:Pr5.1.7a: write rehearsal criteria as a class, rehearse with them and decide when the music is ready. MU:Pr6.1.7a: perform accurately and in style (mento's lift, the Romance's smooth line) to carry out the composer's intent. MU:Pr6.1.7b: show performer and audience manners that fit context, purpose and venue (a samul nori crowd outdoors, a recital, the school concert), dress included, taught as facts. Words: style, class criteria, feedback, ready, context, venue. Showing: the class concert in groups for another class or families, with the family's yes. End-of-unit evidence (the class concert in groups on the check day, day 125), id by id: MU:Cr3.1.7a, a kept revision note: the draft judged on the criteria the student chose; MU:Cr3.1.7b, the same note: each change with its reason from the teacher's and a peer's feedback; MU:Cr3.2.7a, a guide look-for: the final score presented, unity and variety clear; MU:Pr5.1.7a, a kept readiness card made on the last rehearsal day: the class criteria checked and the decision with a reason; MU:Pr6.1.7a, a guide look-for: "Mango Walk" or the piece played accurately and in its style; MU:Pr6.1.7b, a guide look-for on stage and audience manners, and a short spoken item after the group's turn: how manners differ between a samul nori crowd and a recital. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: the student's own criteria, practice true to a culture (unit 12). Tools: the revision note, the class criteria chart, the readiness card, Curio Class's own. Free path: as unit 4. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in grade 7 because the concert presents unit 7's piece and the styles read in unit 5. Its concert is its own end-of-unit assessment (day 125, week 31); the year-end check in week 32 samples units 5 to 7 | Candidate: the class criteria chart written so it carries into year 8's own criteria | Samul nori: told from a Korean national cultural body's or a museum's page, read first by the agent; pungmul's roots in village rites are named and studied by listening only, and no student plays a ritual piece. Performing: as unit 4. Hazards and controls: as unit 4. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 9 | 8 | Whole programs. *How are contrasting pieces built, how do we read new music at sight, and how does a whole concert carry its setting?* | 18 | mu-g8-read; mu-g8-respond | MU:Pr4.2.8a; MU:Pr4.2.8b; MU:Pr4.2.8c; MU:Re7.2.8a; MU:Re7.2.8b; MU:Re9.1.8a; MU:Cn11.1.8a | Performing (Pr4); responding (Re7, Re9); connecting (Cn11). Works: "Londonderry Air" (Ireland, a traditional tune first printed in 1855) and "SambalelA�" (Brazil, a traditional song), the class's two contrasting performance pieces from Curio Class lead sheets; three programs compared: a United States Marine Band concert (federal recordings in the public domain, the band's printed program); a concert by Fairuz (Lebanon, born in the 1930s; the unit plan records her dates and whether living), described and linked; a fado concert program by Mariza (Portugal, born 1973 in Mozambique; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: reading form and tempo signs on two contrasting pieces (mu-g7-read, unit 5) and comparing settings with chosen criteria (mu-g7-respond, unit 5). MU:Pr4.2.8a: compare how the two contrasting pieces are built, explaining how the elements work in each. MU:Pr4.2.8b: sight-read simple rhythm, melody or harmony lines on treble or bass staff (new eight-bar Curio Class lines; the bass staff taught here, so a changing voice can read its part). MU:Pr4.2.8c: name how each piece's culture and history shape its performance and create different effects (samba's roots in Bahia; a ballad's free tempo). MU:Re7.2.8a: compare how elements and expression connect to structure across the three whole programs (an opening, the order, a close). MU:Re7.2.8b: name and compare the settings of the programs, across genres, cultures and eras. MU:Re9.1.8a: write fitting criteria of the student's own and judge a piece or performance with them. MU:Cn11.1.8a: show how a program relates to another art, a subject, a setting or daily life. Technique: sight