293:| 3 | 6 | AB and ABA. *How do composers shape a short piece from start to finish, and how do we write it so someone else can play it?* | 12 | mu-g6-invent; mu-g6-document | MU:Cr1.1.6a; MU:Cr2.1.6a; MU:Cr2.1.6b | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky, "Dance of the Sugar Plum Fairy" from The Nutcracker (Russia, 1892), an ABA piece, a public domain score in a checked recording; "Down in the Valley" (United States, a traditional song of Kentucky), sung by the class, a tune on two chords (I and V); Astor Piazzolla (Argentina, 1921 to 1992), "Libertango" (1974), a tango built on one repeated phrase, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the idea bank (mu-g5-invent) and notating a two-chord harmony (mu-g5-document), each with its reteach. MU:Cr1.1.6a: compose short rhythm, melody and harmony phrases (a four-bar question and a four-bar answer over I and V) in AB or ABA form for a stated mood or aim. MU:Cr2.1.6a: document the piece as AB or ABA with a clear start, middle and end, the sections labeled on the score. MU:Cr2.1.6b: document the two-chord ideas and the rhythm and melody phrases in standard notation on staff paper, with chord letters above (a sound-only take beside it where a device exists). Technique: the question-and-answer phrase ending on the home note; chord roots under the tune. Words: phrase, AB form, ABA form, intent, harmony, chord. Showing: each pair's draft piece played for a partner pair, then the class (the final, revised version is unit 4's). End-of-unit evidence (one check day, day 49: each pair hands its score to another pair, which plays four bars from it at the teacher's spot), id by id: MU:Cr1.1.6a, a guide look-for on the kept composition: phrases in AB or ABA form that fit the stated aim, written in one line at the top; MU:Cr2.1.6a, a guide look-for on the same score: a clear start, middle and end, the sections labeled; MU:Cr2.1.6b, a guide look-for: rhythm, melody and the two chords notated so the other pair can play four bars from it. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: judging against criteria and the final presentation (unit 4), a chord pattern of four (unit 7). Tools: staff paper, the phrase step card, the chord-root card, Curio Class's own; a notation tool on a shared device or the paper score. Free path: the chord roots sung by one group. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 2 because composing for a stated intent uses the planning of intent just practiced, and the notation uses unit 1's symbols; before unit 4, which revises and presents this piece. The idea-bank and notation-ladder models are retrieved here. 12 days: three ids, one product drafted, critiqued and revised | Candidate: a model ABA piece by a living composer from Asia or Africa, described and linked | "Down in the Valley": the agent checks its published history and verses first and keeps only verses fit for school. Piazzolla: the tango's history in Buenos Aires is told from a museum's or an archive's page, read first by the agent; no student dances the tango (dance is a different course). Pair work: a student who works better alone may compose alone and play with a partner at the check. Hazards and controls: tuned bells or a keyboard shared by pairs, wiped between classes; soft playing; hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
294:| 4 | 6 | Ready for the stage. *How do we know when our music is ready, and how do performers and audiences fit the place they are in?* | 12 | mu-g6-revise; mu-g6-perform | MU:Cr3.1.6a; MU:Cr3.1.6b; MU:Cr3.2.6a; MU:Pr5.1.6a; MU:Pr6.1.6a; MU:Pr6.1.6b | Creating (Cr3); performing (Pr5, Pr6); responding (review, with the teacher's criteria). Works: a powwow's published etiquette (a tribal nation's or a powwow committee's own page, picked by the unit plan), with a social song by a living drum group, described and linked: how dancers, singers and the audience act, and the parts of a powwow that are ceremonial; a United States Marine Band concert recording (public domain) and the band's own concert etiquette page: a concert hall; the class's own ABA pieces from unit 3 and "Shady Grove" from unit 1 | Retrieval in day 2's opener: a revision with reasons (mu-g5-revise) and a rehearsal log (mu-g5-perform), each with its reteach. MU:Cr3.1.6a: judge the unit 3 piece against the teacher's criteria (the chosen elements, the sound sources). MU:Cr3.1.6b: explain in writing why each revision was made, from the teacher's feedback and the criteria. MU:Cr3.2.6a: present the final notated piece, shaped from start to finish, with craft and an idea of the pair's own. MU:Pr5.1.6a: rehearse with the teacher's criteria and decide with a readiness check when a piece is ready. MU:Pr6.1.6a: perform a piece (the pair's own or another pair's) accurately, to carry out its composer's intent. MU:Pr6.1.6b: show performer and audience manners that fit the venue and purpose (a concert hall, a powwow, the school assembly), including what each setting expects people to wear, taught as facts, never by costume. Words: criteria, revise, ready, accurate, stage presence, venue. Showing: the class concert in groups for another class or families, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (the class concert in groups on the check day, day 61), id by id: MU:Cr3.1.6a, a kept revision note: the draft judged on the teacher's criteria, line by line; MU:Cr3.1.6b, the same note: each change with its reason from the feedback; MU:Cr3.2.6a, a guide look-for: the final score presented, sections clear from start to finish; MU:Pr5.1.6a, a kept readiness card made on the last rehearsal day: the criteria checked and the decision "ready" or "one more rehearsal", with a reason; MU:Pr6.1.6a, a guide look-for: performed accurately, as the score shows; MU:Pr6.1.6b, a guide look-for on the group's stage presence and its manners as audience for the other groups, and a short spoken item after its turn: one way manners differ between the powwow and the concert hall. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: criteria the student chooses, playing in style (unit 8). Tools: the revision note, the readiness card, the concert plan card, Curio Class's own. Free path: the class's pieces, the voice, found instruments. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in grade 6 because the concert presents the year's composed piece (unit 3) and pieces read and interpreted in units 1 and 2. The critique-protocol and practice-plan models are retrieved here. Its concert is its own end-of-unit assessment (day 61, week 31); the year-end check in week 32 samples units 1 to 3 | Candidate: the readiness card written so a student can use it again in years 7 and 8 | Powwow: the Grand Entry, honor songs and some dances are ceremonial; the unit names them and studies them by watching and listening only, from the nation's or committee's own pages, read first by the sensitivity agent; no student dances, drums or sings a powwow song, wears regalia or imitates it, and a school may leave the powwow study out (Leave-out bullet). Performing: groups only; no solo unless chosen; a student who does not want to be recorded is heard live. A student's own piece is shown outside class only with the family's yes and under the student's chosen name. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; the front of the room cleared, no cables across the aisle. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
295:| 5 | 7 | Two pieces, two worlds. *How are two contrasting pieces built, and why do musicians from different places and times play the same signs differently?* | 18 | mu-g7-read; mu-g7-respond | MU:Pr4.2.7a; MU:Pr4.2.7b; MU:Pr4.2.7c; MU:Re7.2.7a; MU:Re7.2.7b; MU:Re9.1.7a; MU:Cn11.1.7a | Performing (Pr4); responding (Re7, Re9); connecting (Cn11). Works: "The Water Is Wide" (Scotland and England, a traditional song), slow and smooth, and "Mango Walk" (Jamaica, a traditional mento song), quick and syncopated, the class's two contrasting performance pieces from Curio Class lead sheets; Antonio Vivaldi, "Spring" from The Four Seasons (Italy, published 1725), printed with a sonnet for each season, a public domain score in a checked recording; the pipa player Wu Man (born in China, works in the United States; the unit plan records her dates and whether living), a traditional pipa piece in sections that speed up, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: naming each symbol on a score (mu-g6-read, unit 1) and naming settings across genres (mu-g6-respond, unit 1). MU:Pr4.2.7a: show and explain how the two contrasting pieces are structured (a strophic ballad against verse and chorus) and how their elements work. MU:Pr4.2.7b: read and name the standard symbols for form (repeat signs, first and second endings, D.C. al fine), tempo (adagio, allegro, ritardando), dynamics, articulation, pitch and rhythm (ties, syncopation). MU:Pr4.2.7c: name how each piece's culture and history shape its performance and lead to different readings (mento's dance beat; a ballad sung slow or quicker by different singers; Vivaldi played on old instruments and on modern ones). MU:Re7.2.7a: sort and explain how elements and expression relate to how contrasting listening works are built (Vivaldi's returning tutti against the pipa piece's sections speeding up). MU:Re7.2.7b: name and compare the settings of music from several genres, cultures and eras. MU:Re9.1.7a: choose criteria from the teacher's list and judge pieces or performances with them. MU:Cn11.1.7a: show how music relates to another art, a subject, a setting or daily life (Vivaldi's sonnets and his music, line by line). Technique: reading form and tempo signs on a lead sheet; syncopation clapped and played. Words: contrast, form, tempo, syncopation, setting, criteria. Showing: the two contrasting pieces sung and played for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days: day 80 the reading sitting, day 81 the listening sitting), id by id: MU:Pr4.2.7a, a kept sheet made on the last rehearsal day: both scores marked with their form and a sentence on how the elements work in each; MU:Pr4.2.7b, a written item: ten symbols on an unseen Curio Class original score, form and tempo signs among them, each named; MU:Pr4.2.7c, a written item: how culture and history lead to two different readings of one of the pieces; MU:Re7.2.7a, a written item on two unseen contrasting recordings: how elements and expression relate to how each is built; MU:Re7.2.7b, a written item: the settings of two unseen excerpts named from their cards and compared; MU:Re9.1.7a, a written item: two criteria chosen from the teacher's list, and an unseen take judged with them; MU:Cn11.1.7a, a written item: one tie between a unit work and another art, a subject, a setting or daily life. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: reading at sight, whole programs (unit 9). Tools: symbol cards, lead sheets, work cards and the listening-log page, Curio Class's own. Free path: as unit 1. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 7 because the year's interpretation (unit 6) is of these two contrasting pieces, and the form signs read here are written in unit 7. Contrast is the grade 7 step in reading and responding alike. 18 days: seven ids, two techniques, two check days | Candidate: a contrasting piece by a living Native American composer, for the year's range | Mento: the agent reads a Jamaican cultural body's or a museum's page on mento first, and checks the verses kept; no accent is put on. Pipa: the piece's story (often a battle or a scene) is told in one plain sentence from a museum's or a university's published words, read first by the agent. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; syncopated bucket playing kept at the set soft stroke. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
