docs\courses\music-grades-3-to-5\frame.md:292:| 9 | 5 | Music and history. *How do history and culture shape the way music is performed and heard, and how do we back a judgment with evidence?* | 16 | mu-g5-choose; mu-g5-respond | MU:Pr4.1.5a; MU:Re7.1.5a; MU:Cn10.1.5a; MU:Re7.2.5a; MU:Re9.1.5a; MU:Cn11.1.5a; MU:Pr4.2.5c | Performing (Pr4); responding (Re7, Re9); connecting (Cn10, Cn11). Works: Miriam Makeba (South Africa, 1932 to 2008), "Pata Pata" (recorded 1967), described and linked to an archive's or a museum's page: a singer who performed for decades in exile; Ludwig van Beethoven, Symphony No. 9, its last movement (Germany, 1824), a public domain score in a checked recording, beside the concert Leonard Bernstein led in Berlin in December 1989 after the Wall opened, described and linked; the song menu: "La Bamba" (Mexico, a traditional son jarocho of Veracruz), "Greensleeves" (England, sixteenth century) and "Bahay Kubo" (the Philippines, a traditional Tagalog song) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the class's skill as a reason (mu-g4-choose) and the cultural setting explained (mu-g4-respond). MU:Pr4.1.5a: choose the class's concert piece, weighing interest, knowledge, the setting and the student's and others' skill. MU:Re7.1.5a: show and explain, with evidence from the music, how a chosen piece reflects interests, experiences, purposes and contexts. MU:Cn10.1.5a: show how the student's tastes, knowledge and skills shape a choice in music, or an invented or public person's, marked the same. MU:Re7.2.5a: cite evidence from the music for how form, elements and the social, cultural or historical setting shape the response. MU:Re9.1.5a: judge a piece or performance by set criteria, citing evidence from its elements for its fit to the setting. MU:Pr4.2.5c: explain how the history, culture and society behind a piece shape its performances (Makeba's songs sung from exile; Beethoven's finale sung in Berlin in 1989). MU:Cn11.1.5a: show how music relates to another art, a subject or daily life. Technique: a written response with a claim, two pieces of evidence from the music and a link, built in steps; the concert piece learned by ear. Words: evidence, claim, history, culture, criteria, context. Showing: the chosen piece sung for a partner class, with the written case for it read aloud by volunteers. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr4.1.5a, a kept card: the pick and its reasons, one of them the skills of the student and others; MU:Re7.1.5a, a listening-log page: how one chosen piece reflects a context, with one piece of evidence from the music; MU:Cn10.1.5a, a talk item: one taste, piece of knowledge or skill and the choice it shapes (own, invented or public, marked the same); MU:Re7.2.5a, a written item on an unseen open recording: a claim about the response with two pieces of evidence from its form or elements and its setting; MU:Re9.1.5a, a written item on the same recording: set criteria marked with evidence from an element for its fit to the setting; MU:Pr4.2.5c, a written item: one way history or culture shaped a performance of one of the unit's two works; MU:Cn11.1.5a, a talk item: one tie between an unseen piece and another art, subject or daily life. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: settings across genres and eras compared (grade 6). Tools: the claim-and-evidence frame, the criteria card, the menu cards and the listening-log page, Curio Class's own. Free path: the printed frame. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 5 because it reopens the listening routine and adds evidence and history, which units 10 and 11 tie to their making; the piece chosen here is unit 12's concert piece. Seven ids, most written, so 16 days | Candidate: a check that the written response's steps fit the reading load (one short card) | Makeba: apartheid and her exile are told in plain sentences from an archive's or a museum's published words, read first by the sensitivity agent; no role play, and no student is asked about their own family's history. The Berlin concert: the Wall is told as a border that divided a city; Bernstein's change of "Freude" (joy) to "Freiheit" (freedom) is the evidence studied. The class sings no sacred text, and "Greensleeves" is sung with its secular verses. Own-taste tasks have an invented or public option. Hazards and controls: recordings at a set low level; voice rest. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs\courses\music-grades-3-to-5\frame.md:293:| 10 | 5 | Chords and character. *How do chords, articulation and a piece's history shape the music we make up?* | 12 | mu-g5-invent; mu-g5-express | MU:Cr1.1.5a; MU:Cr1.1.5b; MU:Pr4.3.5a; MU:Re8.1.5a | Creating (Cr1); performing (Pr4); responding (Re8). Works: Arturo Márquez (Mexico, born 1950; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), Danzón No. 2 (1994), a concert piece from the danzón, a dance music of Cuba and of Veracruz, Mexico, described and linked, its rights line checked in the unit plan; a steel orchestra's performance at Panorama, Trinidad and Tobago's steelpan competition, described and linked to a national cultural body's or an archive's page (the steelpan, made from oil drums in Port of Spain in the 1930s and 1940s; the unit plan picks the band and the year) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: harmony over a drone (mu-g4-invent) and tone color explained (mu-g4-express). MU:Cr1.1.5a: improvise rhythm, melody and harmony over a I and V pattern, and explain the tie between the ideas and a social, cultural or historical setting (a danzón's dance hall, a steel band's carnival). MU:Cr1.1.5b: invent an accompaniment with easy chords (I and V) in related keys and meters. MU:Pr4.3.5a: plan the loudness, speed, tone color and articulation (smooth or short, accented) of a piece on the expression map, perform it and explain the choices. MU:Re8.1.5a: explain how a recorded performer's choices of loudness, speed, tone color and articulation, and the student's own, carry the music's meaning. Technique: the I and V chords sung in three groups or played on the keyboard or tuned bottles; articulation marks. Words: chord, accompaniment, articulation, accent, history, character. Showing: each group's improvised piece over its chords, for the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Cr1.1.5a, a performance item at the teacher's spot: a phrase improvised over I and V, with one sentence tying it to a setting and one piece of evidence; MU:Cr1.1.5b, a guide look-for on the kept idea-bank card and its recording: an accompaniment with the two chords in the given key and meter; MU:Pr4.3.5a, a guide look-for on the group's performance and its expression map: the four qualities as planned, with the student's spoken reason; MU:Re8.1.5a, a written item on an unseen open recording: one articulation choice and what it carries. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: AB and ABA form (grade 6). Tools: the expression map, the chord card and the idea-bank card, Curio Class's own; the free on-screen keyboard or the sung chord. Free path: the voice and the tuned bottles. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 9 because tying ideas to a history uses its evidence and history words; before unit 11 because the two-chord harmony written down there starts from the accompaniments here. One technique and four ids, so 12 days | Candidate: a living steelpan arranger named from the national cultural body's page | Steelpan history: earlier drum bans in colonial Trinidad are told in one plain sentence from the cultural body's or archive's page, read first by the sensitivity agent. Hazards and controls: found drums played with soft beaters at a set level, hearing protection allowed, no drum near an ear; tuned bottles plastic, capped and taped by an adult. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs\courses\music-grades-3-to-5\frame.md:294:| 11 | 5 | Well-crafted. *How do composers develop an idea and a two-chord harmony, and why does each revision change the piece?* | 16 | mu-g5-document; mu-g5-revise | MU:Cr2.1.5a; MU:Cr2.1.5b; MU:Cr3.1.5a; MU:Cr3.2.5a | Creating (Cr2, Cr3); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Jerod Impichchaachaaha' Tate (Chickasaw Nation, born 1968; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a composer who writes concert music from Chickasaw history and language; the unit plan picks one work from the composer's own page, described and linked; Liliʻuokalani (Kingdom of Hawaiʻi, 1838 to 1917), "Aloha ʻOe" (1878), one of the many songs she wrote and wrote down, a public domain score heard in a checked recording; "Skip to My Lou" (United States, a play-party song of the 1840s), sung by the class as a two-chord model | Retrieval in day 2's opener: organized ideas with a harmony part (mu-g4-document) and a dated revision log (mu-g4-revise). MU:Cr2.1.5a: play developed ideas for a short arrangement or composition and explain what each is for and its setting. MU:Cr2.1.5b: write down rhythm, melody and a two-chord (I and V) harmony with notation or a recording, by beat counts and half beats (mixed note values as fractions of a bar wait for unit 12). MU:Cr3.1.5a: revise the piece against shared criteria, logging each change with its reason. MU:Cr3.2.5a: share the well-crafted finished piece with an audience and explain how it shows the student's aim. Technique: chord symbols written over the tune; developing a motif by repeating, changing and extending it. Words: develop, motif, chord symbol, harmony, reason, well-crafted. Showing: the composers' share for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Cr2.1.5a, a performance item at the share: the piece played, with a sentence on how one idea was developed and what it is for; MU:Cr2.1.5b, a guide look-for on the kept score (or its recording): rhythm, melody and the I and V chords written so another student can read them; MU:Cr3.1.5a, a guide look-for on the kept revision log: each change with its reason, before and after versions kept; MU:Cr3.2.5a, a talk item after the group's turn: how the finished piece shows the aim, explained. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: AB and ABA form with a planned start, middle and end (grade 6). Tools: staff paper with chord lines, the revision log page and the criteria card, Curio Class's own; a notation tool or the printed staff. Free path: pencil and staff paper. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 10, whose chord accompaniments become the harmony written here; before unit 12 because reading chord symbols in the concert score follows writing them. A product with reasons logged, so 16 days | Candidate: a check that the Tate work picked is the composer's concert music and not a ceremonial song | Chickasaw music: the sensitivity agent first reads the composer's own page and the Chickasaw Nation's published pages, and the class studies the work by listening only. Liliʻuokalani: the 1893 overthrow of the Hawaiian Kingdom and her imprisonment are told in plain sentences from a Hawaiian archive's or museum's published words, read first by the agent; the song is heard, not sung, unless the agent's read finds it open to be taught. Hazards and controls: shared instruments wiped and hands washed; the share in groups, never a forced solo. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs\courses\music-grades-3-to-5\frame.md:295:| 12 | 5 | Our band-year concert. *How do we read a piece in standard notation and grow it over weeks of rehearsal for its venue?* | 16 | mu-g5-read; mu-g5-perform | MU:Pr4.2.5a; MU:Pr4.2.5b; MU:Pr5.1.5a; MU:Pr5.1.5b; MU:Pr6.1.5a; MU:Pr6.1.5b | Performing (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6); responding practiced on the model work (review). Works: the concert piece chosen in unit 9, in a Curio Class two-part arrangement with I and V chords; the Simón Bolívar Symphony Orchestra of Venezuela, led by Gustavo Dudamel (born 1981), playing Márquez's Danzón No. 2 from unit 10, revisited, described and linked to a broadcaster's or the orchestra's page: how a youth orchestra's style and stage manners fit the piece and the hall | Retrieval in day 2's opener: timing, pitch and form explained (mu-g4-read) and shared criteria with an interpretation for the genre (mu-g4-perform). MU:Pr4.2.5a: show how timing, pitch, form and harmony work in the concert piece, on its score and form map. MU:Pr4.2.5b: read and perform it from standard notation, adding mixed note values within a bar. MU:Pr5.1.5a: judge accuracy and expression in group and solo takes with the teacher's criteria. MU:Pr5.1.5b: rehearse across several weeks, keeping takes in a rehearsal log that show growth. MU:Pr6.1.5a: sing or play alone or in a group with expression, accuracy and a fitting interpretation. MU:Pr6.1.5b: show performer and audience manners that suit the style, genre, venue and setting. Words: harmony, dotted note, chord symbol, style, rehearsal log, venue. Showing: the band-year concert for families or the school, with the family's yes. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MU:Pr4.2.5a, a written item on the reading day: one way the harmony and the form work in the concert piece, on its marked score; MU:Pr4.2.5b, a performance item at the teacher's spot on the reading day: an unseen four-bar line in standard notation read and played; MU:Pr5.1.5a, a kept card: a group take and a solo or doubled take marked on the teacher's criteria; MU:Pr5.1.5b, a guide look-for on the kept rehearsal log: three dated takes and what changed between them; MU:Pr6.1.5a, a guide look-for at the concert: the part accurate and expressive, its interpretation fitting; MU:Pr6.1.5b, a guide look-for at the concert: manners as performer and as audience for this style and venue. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: deciding alone when a piece is ready (grade 6). Tools: the marked score, form map, rehearsal log and criteria card, reading cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: the voice and printed cards. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | Last in the band because the concert ends it after the year-end check (week 24); mixed note values within a bar lean on math grade 5 unit 9 (days 119 to 134, school weeks 24 to 27), which this unit meets from week 25, with its "if later" line. The rhythm-syllables and practice-plan models are retrieved here | Candidate: the rehearsal log's three takes kept as sound only, on the school's device | A solo is offered, never required; every part has a doubled version for the home room. Family yes for any showing outside class; a student's name is printed only as the student chooses. Hazards and controls: voice rest and water; no loud drum near an ear; the concert space cleared and steps checked. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
docs\courses\music-grades-3-to-5\frame.md:305:| Year 5 mid-year (week 15, days 157 and 158) | 10 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 9 and 10: a written card on an unseen open recording with a claim and two pieces of evidence for how its elements and setting shape the response, and its fit by set criteria (MU:Re7.2.5a, MU:Re9.1.5a, unit 9), and at the teacher's spot a new accompaniment on I and V in a given key and meter (MU:Cr1.1.5b, unit 10). Unit 10's idea-bank and expression-map record (day 155) is carried unscored. Respond on day 158. |
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:1:# Course frame: Music, grades 9 to 12
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:2:
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:3:Course `music-grades-9-to-12` (catalogue id `music-9-12`), a band course for high school run as levels, not grades: Level 1 (Proficient), Level 2 (Accomplished), Level 3 (Advanced, at honors depth) and an optional Level 4 (Advanced, deepened, a portfolio year the student leads), one level a school year (STANDARD.md 3.2). It is a composition and theory course on the national composition and theory strand (STANDARD.md 3.3). Draft frame, written 2026-11-25; the scope and sequence sets the days later, one level at a time, and each level's part of this file is that level's frame.
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:4:
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:5:## The course in brief
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:6:
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:193:| mu-g8-invent | mu-g9-invent (unit 2) | Composes from an outside idea (a mood, image or story) with no set form, where grade 8 built phrases inside a given expanded form |
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:194:| mu-g8-document | mu-g9-document (unit 3) | Traces a short idea across three or more dated drafts, where grade 8 documented one finished piece |
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:195:| mu-g8-revise | mu-g9-revise (unit 4) | Revises with the teacher's criteria and adds the setting and medium to the revision note |
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:196:| mu-g8-read | mu-g9-read (unit 3) | Analyzes model works by ear for composing, where grade 8 read and compared scores to perform them |
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:197:| mu-g8-express | mu-g9-express (unit 4) | Writes an interpretation citing genre and technique as evidence for the composer's aim |
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:198:| mu-g8-perform | mu-g9-perform (unit 5) | Writes a rehearsal plan and performs the student's own and others' works, live or recorded |
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:199:| mu-g8-choose | mu-g9-choose (unit 2) | Chooses passages as models for composing, logging how tastes and skills shaped each pick |
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:200:| mu-g8-respond | mu-g9-respond (unit 5) | Critiques with basic theory terms and says what a classmate's feedback changes in the student's own piece |
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:232:| idea-bank | Grade 8 (mu-g8-invent, model_out) | Unit 2: the idea bank of short sound ideas for a mood, image or story (bridge unit 1 retrieves its grade 8 form) | Unit 7 (memories, texts, concepts), unit 12 (abstract and extended ideas), unit 16 (the portfolio proposal) |
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:233:| notation-ladder | Grade 8 (mu-g8-document, model_out) | Unit 1 (bridge): staff reading and writing retaught; unit 3: dated drafts in a simple form | Unit 8 (rondo and ternary), unit 13 (complex forms), unit 16 (portfolio drafts) |
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:234:| critique-protocol | Grade 8 (mu-g8-revise, model_out) | Unit 1 (bridge): describe, ask, suggest retaught; unit 4: revision with the teacher's criteria | Unit 9 (own criteria), unit 14 (researched criteria), unit 16; every unit's critique as review |
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:235:| rhythm-syllables | Grade 8 (mu-g8-read, model_out) | Unit 1 (bridge): rhythm syllables and solfege for reading; unit 3: by-ear analysis and dictation | Unit 8 (score analysis), unit 13 (defended analysis), unit 17 (reviews); every opener's sight-singing as review |
docs\courses\music-grades-9-to-12\frame.md:236:| expression-map | Grade 8 (mu-g8-express, model_out) | Unit 4: the map of elements, style and mood against the composer's aim | Unit 9 (function and setting added), unit 14 (two readings set side by side), units 17 and 19 |
