howing: final versions shared at the class showing. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Re8.1.C.Ia, a sitting item: a short written interpretation of an unseen work's aim, citing one technique and its genre; MU:Pr4.3.C.Ia, a look-for on the kept expression map: performing choices tied to the aim; MU:Cr3.1.C.Ia, a look-for on the draft trail: each change tied to a teacher criterion; MU:Cr3.2.C.Ia, a look-for on the shared final version and its note: elements named as serving the aim; MU:Cr3.2.C.Ib, a written note on the last making day: how the setting and medium shaped the piece. Mid-unit assessment: the expression map, near day 98. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: self-chosen criteria and settings (Level 2). Tools: the expression map and the revision checklist, Curio Class's own | After the mid-year check because revision needs unit 3's drafts; interpretation of others' works comes first so the student asks of their own piece what they asked of the composers'. Before unit 5 because the concert performs final versions | Candidate: a European or Asian work for range beside the three American works | Tate's work tells Chickasaw history and stories: the sensitivity agent reads the composer's own pages and the Chickasaw Nation's published pages first; the unit studies it by listening and from the composer's words only, and no student imitates Chickasaw song, language or dress. Price is taught with her setting (Arkansas, Chicago, the first symphony by a Black woman composer played by a major American orchestra, 1933, as the orchestra's or archive's page states it). Hazards and controls: voice warm-up and rest; no singing alone required. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (voice and sound). |
