134:mu,4,mu-g4-invent,MU:Cr1.1.4a; MU:Cr1.1.4b,mu-g3-invent,mu-g5-invent,idea-bank,idea-bank,harmony; accompaniment; related; explain; setting; drone,,"Improvise rhythms and tunes with a simple harmony part over a drone, say how they fit a stated purpose, and make up a short accompaniment in a given key and meter. What changes: harmony and accompaniment join rhythm and melody. Assessed by a performance with a spoken reason, scored on a short rubric."
135:mu,4,mu-g4-document,MU:Cr2.1.4a; MU:Cr2.1.4b,mu-g3-document,mu-g5-document,notation-ladder,notation-ladder,arrange; compose; organized; context; harmony; notation,,"Play ideas chosen and organized for improvising, arranging or composing, say what each is for, and write down rhythm, melody and a simple harmony part. What changes: organizing and explaining join documenting, and simple harmony is written down, where grade 3 documented one melody. Assessed by a documented piece and a spoken or written reason, scored on a rubric."
136:mu,4,mu-g4-revise,MU:Cr3.1.4a; MU:Cr3.2.4a,mu-g3-revise,mu-g5-revise,critique-protocol,critique-protocol,growth; shared criteria; revise; record; audience; explain,,"Keep a revision log for one piece against shared criteria, recording each change and the date, then share the finished piece with an audience and say how it shows the student's aim. What changes: changes are kept over time and shown as growth, where grade 3 kept a change list. Assessed by a portfolio entry with before and after versions, shared with an audience with its intent explained, scored on a rubric."
137:mu,4,mu-g4-read,MU:Pr4.2.4a; MU:Pr4.2.4b; MU:Pr4.2.4c,mu-g3-read,mu-g5-read,rhythm-syllables,rhythm-syllables,timing; pitch; form; time signature; notation; culture,"math:4.NF.A.1 (equal note values, such as two quarters in one half)","Mark the rhythm, pitch and form of a piece the class will perform, read and play it from picture or standard notation, and explain how its social and cultural setting shapes how it is sung or played. Equal note values are shown as equal parts of a bar. What changes: elements are named and explained, and setting is explained rather than described. Assessed by a marked score and form map, a read-and-play task and a short explanation, scored on a rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
138:mu,4,mu-g4-express,MU:Pr4.3.4a; MU:Re8.1.4a,mu-g3-express,mu-g5-express,expression-map,expression-map,interpretive choice; tone color; loudness; speed; intent; explain,,"Plan the loudness, speed and tone color of a song on an expression map and perform it, then explain how a recorded performer's choices carry the music's meaning. What changes: tone color joins loudness and speed, and explaining replaces describing. Assessed by a performance and a spoken reason scored on a rubric."
139:mu,4,mu-g4-perform,MU:Pr5.1.4a; MU:Pr5.1.4b; MU:Pr6.1.4a; MU:Pr6.1.4b,mu-g3-perform,mu-g5-perform,practice-plan,practice-plan,solo; group; shared criteria; interpretation; genre; manners,,"Judge group and solo takes with a shared checklist, rehearse to fix accuracy and expression, and perform alone or in a group with the interpretation and manners the setting and genre call for. What changes: interpretation is tied to the genre, where grade 3 tied manners to the venue. Assessed by a performance, a take judged on class criteria and a self-check, with performer and audience manners observed, scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: a solo or doubled version is always offered."
140:mu,4,mu-g4-choose,MU:Pr4.1.4a; MU:Re7.1.4a; MU:Cn10.1.4a,mu-g3-choose,mu-g5-choose,listening-log,listening-log,technical skill; setting; context; reflect; choose; explain,,"Choose a piece for the class to perform and argue for it (interest, what the class can play, where it will be heard), and tell what in the student's own experience the pick reflects. What changes: the child's own technical skill is a reason for choosing, where grade 3 weighed setting. Assessed by a listening-log entry with a reason scored on a short rubric."
141:mu,4,mu-g4-respond,MU:Re7.2.4a; MU:Re9.1.4a; MU:Cn11.1.4a,mu-g3-respond,mu-g5-respond,describe-interpret-judge,describe-interpret-judge,structure; element; culture; criteria; explain; connect,,"Listen to a piece, describe how its form, elements and social or cultural setting shape the response, score it with set criteria and say whether it fits its setting, and link it to another art or subject. What changes: explaining replaces describing: the child says why the setting changes the response. Assessed by a listen-and-explain response scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
142:mu,5,mu-g5-invent,MU:Cr1.1.5a; MU:Cr1.1.5b,mu-g4-invent,mu-g6-invent,idea-bank,idea-bank,context; culture; history; chords; accompaniment; society,,"Improvise rhythm, melody and harmony, explain the tie between the ideas and a social, cultural or historical setting, and invent an accompaniment with easy chords. What changes: ideas are tied to a social, cultural or historical setting, and easy chords back them. Assessed by a performance and a short explanation with one piece of evidence, scored on a rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
143:mu,5,mu-g5-document,MU:Cr2.1.5a; MU:Cr2.1.5b,mu-g4-document,mu-g6-document,notation-ladder,notation-ladder,developed; two-chord; arrange; context; notation; recording,,"Develop ideas for an improvisation, arrangement or short composition, say what each is for, and notate the rhythm, the melody and a two-chord harmony. What changes: ideas are developed further and a two-chord harmony is written, where grade 4 wrote a simple harmony part. Assessed by a documented piece, played with its purpose and setting explained, scored on a rubric for development and clear notation."
144:mu,5,mu-g5-revise,MU:Cr3.1.5a; MU:Cr3.2.5a,mu-g4-revise,mu-g6-revise,critique-protocol,critique-protocol,reasons; criteria; revise; well-crafted; audience; growth,,"Revise a piece against shared criteria, logging each change with its reason, then share the polished version with an audience and explain how it shows the student's aim. What changes: the child explains why each change was made. Assessed by a portfolio entry with before and after versions and reasons, shared with an audience with its intent explained, scored on a rubric."
145:mu,5,mu-g5-read,MU:Pr4.2.5a; MU:Pr4.2.5b,mu-g4-read,mu-g6-read,rhythm-syllables,rhythm-syllables,harmony; timing; pitch; form; staff; notation,math:5.NF.A.1 (adding mixed note values within a bar),"Show how timing, pitch, form and harmony work in a piece chosen to perform, and read and perform it from standard notation. Mixed note values are added within a bar. What changes: harmony joins the elements and standard notation is now the default, where grade 4 used picture or standard notation. Assessed by a read-and-play task and a written explanation scored on a rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
146:mu,5,mu-g5-express,MU:Pr4.3.5a; MU:Re8.1.5a,mu-g4-express,mu-g6-express,expression-map,expression-map,articulation; tone color; interpretive choice; intent; explain; performer,,"Plan the dynamics, tempo, tone color and articulation of a piece on an expression map and perform it, then explain how a recorded performer's choices carry the music's meaning. What changes: articulation joins the qualities explained, where grade 4 explained tone color. Assessed by a performance and a written explanation scored on a rubric."
147:mu,5,mu-g5-perform,MU:Pr5.1.5a; MU:Pr5.1.5b; MU:Pr6.1.5a; MU:Pr6.1.5b,mu-g4-perform,mu-g6-perform,practice-plan,practice-plan,growth; criteria; genre; style; interpretation; venue,,"Rehearse a piece against the teacher's checklist across several weeks, keeping takes that show growth, and perform alone or in a group with manners that fit the style, genre and venue. What changes: growth over time is shown across rehearsals with a log, where grade 4 judged by shared criteria. Assessed by a performance, takes judged on the teacher's criteria and a rehearsal log, with performer and audience manners observed, scored on a rubric. Teacher note: a solo or doubled version is always offered."
148:mu,5,mu-g5-choose,MU:Pr4.1.5a; MU:Re7.1.5a; MU:Cn10.1.5a,mu-g4-choose,mu-g6-choose,listening-log,listening-log,evidence; interest; skill; setting; context; choose,,"Choose a piece to perform, weighing interest, what the student and others can play and where it will be heard, and back the choice with evidence of what it reflects. What changes: claims come with evidence from the music. Assessed by a listening-log entry with evidence scored on a rubric."
149:mu,5,mu-g5-respond,MU:Re7.2.5a; MU:Re9.1.5a; MU:Cn11.1.5a; MU:Pr4.2.5c,mu-g4-respond,mu-g6-respond,describe-interpret-judge,describe-interpret-judge,evidence; history; culture; criteria; setting; connect,"ela:W.4.1 (an opinion supported by reasons, taught the year before)","Write a short response to a piece backed by evidence from the music: how its form, elements and setting shape the student's reaction, how it scores on set criteria for fit to its setting, and how history and culture shape its performance, then link it to another art, subject or setting. What changes: evidence from the music is required, in the response and in the judgment, and the setting of a performance is tied to history and society. Assessed by a written response with evidence scored on a rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
150:mu,6,mu-g6-invent,MU:Cr1.1.6a,mu-g5-invent,mu-g7-invent,idea-bank,idea-bank,phrase; AB form; ABA form; intent; harmony; chord,,"Compose short rhythm, melody and harmony phrases in AB or ABA form for a stated mood or aim. What changes: phrases are shaped into AB or ABA form to carry an intent. Assessed by a short composed piece scored on a rubric for form and intent."
151:mu,6,mu-g6-document,MU:Cr2.1.6a; MU:Cr2.1.6b,mu-g5-document,mu-g7-document,notation-ladder,notation-ladder,AB form; ABA form; beginning; middle; end; two chords,,"Notate an AB or ABA piece with a clear start, middle and end, using two-chord harmony and simple rhythm and melody phrases. What changes: pieces have a planned shape from start to finish, where grade 5 documented developed ideas. Assessed by a documented piece scored on a rubric for shape and clear notation or audio."
152:mu,6,mu-g6-revise,MU:Cr3.1.6a; MU:Cr3.1.6b; MU:Cr3.2.6a,mu-g5-revise,mu-g7-revise,critique-protocol,critique-protocol,criteria; sound source; music element; revise; craft; originality,,"Revise a piece against the teacher's criteria, write why each change was made from the feedback, and present the notated arrangement or composition. What changes: the student judges the piece and presents a whole documented piece. Assessed by a presented piece and a revision note scored on a rubric for craft, originality and reasons."
153:mu,6,mu-g6-read,MU:Pr4.2.6a; MU:Pr4.2.6b; MU:Pr4.2.6c,mu-g5-read,mu-g7-read,rhythm-syllables,rhythm-syllables,dynamics; articulation; pitch; rhythm; symbol; culture,,"Study a piece the class will perform, mark its structure and its dynamics, articulation, pitch and rhythm symbols, and say how its culture and history shape the way it is played. What changes: symbols are read and named one by one, where grade 5 read notation without naming each symbol. Assessed by a symbol-reading task and a written note on the piece, scored on a rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
154:mu,6,mu-g6-express,MU:Pr4.3.6a; MU:Re8.1.6a,mu-g5-express,mu-g7-express,expression-map,expression-map,interpretation; genre; history; elements; expression; intent,,"Perform a chosen piece with interpretive choices the student plans, and write a short reading of how another performance's choices, within its genre and history, carry intent. What changes: the student makes interpretive choices alone and describes them, where grade 5 explained choices made together. Assessed by a performance and a written description scored on a rubric."
155:mu,6,mu-g6-perform,MU:Pr5.1.6a; MU:Pr6.1.6a; MU:Pr6.1.6b,mu-g5-perform,mu-g7-perform,practice-plan,practice-plan,ready; criteria; creator; intent; stage presence; dress,,"Rehearse a piece against the teacher's criteria, decide with a readiness check when it is ready, and perform it accurately with stage presence that fits the venue. What changes: the student decides when a piece is ready. Assessed by a performance and a readiness note scored on a rubric."
156:mu,6,mu-g6-choose,MU:Pr4.1.6a; MU:Re7.1.6a; MU:Cn10.1.6a,mu-g5-choose,mu-g7-choose,listening-log,listening-log,criteria; choose; purpose; setting; interest; experience,,"Choose music for a stated purpose or event with the teacher's criteria, explain each pick, and tie one choice to the student's own interests or experience. What changes: teacher criteria guide each pick, where grade 5 chose by evidence from the music. Assessed by a listening-log entry with a reason scored on a rubric. Every personal tie has an invented or public option beside it, marked the same (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground)."
157:mu,6,mu-g6-respond,MU:Re7.2.6a; MU:Re7.2.6b; MU:Re9.1.6a; MU:Cn11.1.6a,mu-g5-respond,mu-g7-respond,describe-interpret-judge,describe-interpret-judge,genre; era; setting; structure; teacher criteria; connect,ela:SL.6.1 (discussion with evidence),"Listen to pieces from several genres, cultures and eras, describe how elements and expression fit each one's structure and name its setting, judge one with the teacher's criteria, and connect it to another art or subject. What changes: settings are named across genres, cultures and eras, where grade 5 cited one setting. Assessed by a written response scored on a rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
166:mu,8,mu-g8-invent,MU:Cr1.1.8a,mu-g7-invent,mu-g9-invent,idea-bank,idea-bank,introduction; transition; coda; expanded form; accompaniment; section,,"Build phrases and accompaniments in an expanded form with an introduction, transitions and a coda. What changes: forms expand with an introduction, transitions and a coda. Assessed by a composed piece scored on a rubric for form, link between sections and accompaniment. Hands on to Level 1 of the high school composition and theory course (STANDARD.md 3.3)."
167:mu,8,mu-g8-document,MU:Cr2.1.8a; MU:Cr2.1.8b,mu-g7-document,mu-g9-document,notation-ladder,notation-ladder,tension; release; balance; expanded form; unity; variety,,"Notate a piece in an expanded form that plans tension and release while keeping unity and variety, with rhythm phrases, melody phrases and a chord sequence. What changes: tension and release are planned and balanced in a longer form. Assessed by a documented piece scored on a rubric for balance and clear documentation."
168:mu,8,mu-g8-revise,MU:Cr3.1.8a; MU:Cr3.1.8b; MU:Cr3.2.8a,mu-g7-revise,mu-g9-revise,critique-protocol,critique-protocol,technique; justify; polish; tension; release; balance,,"Revise a piece with criteria the student picks (technique, style, form, sound sources), justify the final polish against them, and present the notated piece. What changes: the student justifies each choice against criteria. Assessed by a presented piece and a written justification scored on a rubric."
169:mu,8,mu-g8-read,MU:Pr4.2.8a; MU:Pr4.2.8b; MU:Pr4.2.8c,mu-g7-read,mu-g9-read,rhythm-syllables,rhythm-syllables,sight-read; treble staff; bass staff; compare; build; effect,,"Compare how two contrasting performance pieces are built, sight-read simple rhythm, melody or harmony lines on treble or bass staves, and explain how culture and history change the way each is played. What changes: the student reads new notation at sight on treble or bass staves, where grade 7 read familiar pieces. Assessed by a sight-reading task and a written comparison, scored on a rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
170:mu,8,mu-g8-express,MU:Pr4.3.8a; MU:Re8.1.8a,mu-g7-express,mu-g9-express,expression-map,expression-map,program; reading; back; evidence; interpretation; convey,,"Perform contrasting pieces and explain how the student's interpretation carries the music's intent, and write an argument with evidence about how a whole program carries intent. What changes: readings are backed with evidence across a whole program. Assessed by a performance and a written argument with evidence, scored on a rubric."
171:mu,8,mu-g8-perform,MU:Pr5.1.8a; MU:Pr6.1.8a; MU:Pr6.1.8b,mu-g7-perform,mu-g9-perform,practice-plan,practice-plan,own criteria; style; culturally authentic; honor; venue; ready,,"Rehearse with the student's own criteria, decide when the music is ready, and perform it accurately, in style and with practice true to its culture, with manners that fit the event and venue. What changes: the student writes the criteria and authentic practice is added. Assessed by a performance and a readiness note scored on a rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
172:mu,8,mu-g8-choose,MU:Pr4.1.8a; MU:Re7.1.8a; MU:Cn10.1.8a,mu-g7-choose,mu-g9-choose,listening-log,listening-log,program; own criteria; mix; select; interest; contrast,,"Build and explain a program of contrasting music with the student's own criteria, such as a themed mix, and show how it ties to the student's interests. What changes: the student sets the criteria and builds a program. Assessed by a program note scored on a rubric. Every personal tie has an invented or public option beside it, marked the same (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground)."
173:mu,8,mu-g8-respond,MU:Re7.2.8a; MU:Re7.2.8b; MU:Re9.1.8a; MU:Cn11.1.8a,mu-g7-respond,mu-g9-respond,describe-interpret-judge,describe-interpret-judge,program; compare; own criteria; setting; evidence; connect,ela:W.8.1 (an argument supported by evidence),"Compare whole programs of music from many genres, cultures and eras, explaining how elements and expression connect to structure and naming each setting, judge them with criteria the student writes, and link the music to another art or subject. What changes: whole programs are compared and the student writes the criteria, where grade 7 compared pairs of pieces. Assessed by a written argument with evidence scored on a rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
174:mu,9,mu-g9-invent,MU:Cr1.1.C.Ia; MU:Cr2.1.C.Ia,mu-g8-invent,mu-g10-invent,idea-bank,idea-bank,sketch; motif; mood; storyline; represent; aim,,"Pick a mood, image or story, collect short sound ideas that could stand for it in an idea bank, and arrange the best into a first sketch with a one-line aim. What changes: the student composes from an outside idea with no set form, where grade 8 built phrases inside a given expanded form. Assessed by a sketch with a one-line aim, scored on a short rubric for the idea, its link to the aim and range. Level 1 bridge: the first unit teaches the grade 8 techniques and words Level 1 needs, as readiness, and claims no grade 8 id (at most 12 days in the full year, 6 on the 80-day short path, STANDARD.md 3.2)."
175:mu,9,mu-g9-document,MU:Cr2.1.C.Ib,mu-g8-document,mu-g10-document,notation-ladder,notation-ladder,draft; binary; cyclical; one-part; develop; motif,,"Develop a short idea through three or more dated drafts in a simple form (one-part, cyclical or binary) and describe in the draft log how it grew. What changes: the student traces growth across drafts, where grade 8 documented one finished piece. Assessed by a draft log scored on a short rubric."
176:mu,9,mu-g9-revise,MU:Cr3.1.C.Ia; MU:Cr3.2.C.Ia; MU:Cr3.2.C.Ib,mu-g8-revise,mu-g10-revise,critique-protocol,critique-protocol,criteria; final version; medium; context; share; intent,,"Revise drafts to a final version with the teacher's criteria, share it as a score, a performance or a recording, and write a short note on how its elements serve the aim and how the given setting and medium shape it. What changes: the setting and medium join the revision. Assessed by a final version, its draft trail and a short note on setting, scored on a rubric."
177:mu,9,mu-g9-read,MU:Pr4.2.C.Ia; MU:Re7.2.C.Ia,mu-g8-read,mu-g10-read,rhythm-syllables,rhythm-syllables,analyze; form; style; mood; aural; model,,"Listen to model works and mark, by ear, how their elements and form fit the style and mood, then write what that means for rehearsing them and for the student's own composing. What changes: analysis serves composing, where grade 8 read and compared scores to perform them. Assessed by a listening analysis scored on a short rubric."
178:mu,9,mu-g9-express,MU:Pr4.3.C.Ia; MU:Re8.1.C.Ia,mu-g8-express,mu-g10-express,expression-map,expression-map,interpret; genre; intent; technical; expressive; evidence,,"Write an interpretation of a work from its elements, style and mood, naming the composer's aim and backing it with technical and expressive evidence and the genre. What changes: genre and technique are cited as evidence for the aim. Assessed by a short written interpretation scored on a rubric."
179:mu,9,mu-g9-perform,MU:Pr5.1.C.Ia; MU:Pr5.1.C.Ib; MU:Pr5.1.C.Ic; MU:Pr6.1.C.Ia; MU:Pr6.1.C.Ib,mu-g8-perform,mu-g10-perform,practice-plan,practice-plan,rehearsal plan; repetition; variation; recorded; audience; feedback,,"Write a rehearsal plan that marks repetition and variation in a piece, rehearse own and others' works with set criteria and feedback, and share a live or recorded performance with a note on how it suits its audience or setting. What changes: the works performed include the student's own compositions, live or recorded, so no instrument is needed. Assessed by a rehearsal plan and a take with a short note, scored on a rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it. Teacher note: no student is recorded, photographed or filmed without the family's yes; a face or voice is never required (hands, the work or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; kept work stays on the student's, family's or school's own device."
180:mu,9,mu-g9-choose,MU:Pr4.1.C.Ia; MU:Re7.1.C.Ia; MU:Cn10.0.C.Ia,mu-g8-choose,mu-g10-choose,listening-log,listening-log,excerpt; passage; model; select; criteria; interest,,"Use the teacher's criteria to choose passages and short pieces in simple forms that carry a mood, image or story as models for composing, logging each pick and how the student's tastes and skills shaped it. What changes: pieces are chosen as models for the student's own composing. Assessed by a model-piece log with reasons, scored on a short rubric. Model pieces come from more than one culture and era, living composers among them. Every personal tie has an invented or public option beside it, marked the same (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground)."
